{
  // Module format for the TypeScript serverless functions. This file
  // exists to make one specific thing true: what Vercel emits under
  // api/ must be CommonJS.
  //
  // WHY IT IS LOAD-BEARING
  // @vercel/node picks the output format like this:
  //
  //   isEsm = ext === ".mjs" || ext === ".mts"
  //           || (pkg.type === "module" && [".js",".ts",".tsx"].includes(ext))
  //   ...
  //   if (isEsm) { if (tsConfig.compilerOptions.module === undefined)
  //                  tsConfig.compilerOptions.module = "nodenext" }
  //
  // api/package.json deliberately sets "type": "commonjs" (see the
  // _comment there), so isEsm is FALSE for our .ts functions — and the
  // `module` correction only happens when it is TRUE. The tsconfig is
  // then resolved by walking UP from the entrypoint, which without this
  // file lands on the repo root tsconfig.json and its "module":
  // "ESNext".
  //
  // Result: ESM `import` statements emitted into a directory Node
  // treats as CommonJS, so every function died at load with
  // "SyntaxError: Cannot use import statement outside a module",
  // surfacing as FUNCTION_INVOCATION_FAILED. That is exactly what
  // happened to /api/v1/* and /api/mcp on 2026-08-25.
  //
  // Sitting here, this config is found before the root one for every
  // entrypoint under api/, so the emit matches the declared type.
  //
  // Guardrail: tests/api/api_module_format.test.js replicates the
  // resolution above and fails if these two ever disagree again.
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "CommonJS",
    "moduleResolution": "Node",
    "lib": ["ES2022"],
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "allowSyntheticDefaultImports": true,
    "allowJs": true,
    "checkJs": false,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "strict": false
  },
  "include": ["**/*.ts"]
}
